th='A child’s innocence and imagination – small paper boats carry big dreams out into the wide world.',
sum='The speaker is a child who floats paper boats, one by one, down the running stream every day. He writes his name and the name of his village on them in big black letters, hoping that someone in a far-off, strange land will find them and know who he is. He loads the little boats with shiuli flowers from his garden and hopes that these flowers, which bloom at dawn, will reach land safely in the night. When he launches the boats he looks up and sees little clouds with white bulging sails; he imagines that some playmate in the sky sends them down to race with his boats. At night he hides his face in his arms and dreams that his boats sail on and on under the midnight stars, with the fairies of sleep sailing in them, carrying baskets full of dreams. The poem is in free verse (no rhyme scheme); its tone is gentle and wishful.',
